<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tetum-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tetum language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tetun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tetum is an Austronesian language of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a> and, with Portuguese, one of the two official languages of Timor-Leste. Its widely spoken form, Tetun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dili">Dili</a>, absorbed many Portuguese loanwords during centuries of colonial rule and served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> across the territory. The Catholic Church's use of Tetum during the Indonesian occupation strengthened it as a symbol of East Timorese identity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tetum-language.jpg" alt="Tetum language" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. Patrick Fischer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tetum_Terik.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
